# Shared host Caddyfile — synced to /etc/caddy/Caddyfile on the VPS by every
# app's deploy workflow. Holds blocks for ALL apps in the monorepo so a
# deploy of one app does not wipe another's host-Caddy entry.
#
# Host Caddy is intentionally a thin TLS-terminator + reverse-proxy. All
# routing — API, frontend, security headers, cache headers — lives in
# each app's docker Caddy. Keep ONLY HSTS + nosniff + Referrer-Policy at
# this layer (network-level protections that make sense at the TLS edge).
# Everything else — XFO, CSP, cache-control, /api routing, SPA fallback —
# is in the docker Caddy.
#
# Adding a new app:
#   1. Append a new <hostname> { ... reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:<port> } block.
#      Copy an existing block verbatim — the `import cloudflare_origin_tls`
#      line and the `header_up X-Forwarded-For {client_ip}` inside
#      reverse_proxy are REQUIRED. See the global block below for what
#      breaks when the header_up line is omitted.
#   2. Pick a unique loopback port (8094 = MBK, 8092 = MJH, 8096 = MGA,
#      8100 = MRC, 9000 = MinIO).
#   3. Make sure the app's docker compose binds that port on 127.0.0.1.
#   4. Create the DNS record in Cloudflare as PROXIED (orange cloud). A
#      DNS-only record republishes the origin IP and loses the routing
#      redundancy this setup exists to provide.
#
# Note: deploys only sync + `caddy reload` this file when it DIFFERS from
# /etc/caddy/Caddyfile.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ORIGIN IS BEHIND CLOUDFLARE (since 2026-09-07)
#
# Why: AT&T — and any ISP whose transit to DigitalOcean goes via Cogent — was
# black-holing every route into AS14061. All five DO prefixes tested died at
# the same Cogent hop (154.54.41.54), so these apps were unreachable for a
# slice of the public internet while the droplet itself was perfectly healthy
# and serving. Fronting the origin with Cloudflare's anycast edge removes the
# single-transit-path dependency: visitors reach the nearest Cloudflare PoP,
# and Cloudflare reaches the origin over its own transit.
#
# Two consequences are load-bearing and must not be casually reverted:
#   1. TLS at this layer uses a Cloudflare ORIGIN CA certificate, not Let's
#      Encrypt. Origin CA certs are trusted by Cloudflare only — never by
#      browsers. That is correct for a proxied origin, but it means turning
#      the orange cloud OFF for a hostname makes that hostname serve an
#      untrusted certificate. Flip DNS back to proxied, or restore ACME for
#      that block, before going DNS-only.
#   2. Cloudflare's SSL/TLS mode must stay on "Full (strict)". Anything
#      weaker ("Flexible") makes Cloudflare speak plain HTTP to an origin
#      that only serves HTTPS here, producing a redirect loop.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

{
    servers {
        # Cloudflare terminates TLS at its edge and dials this origin from its
        # own address space, so once proxying is on the socket peer is ALWAYS a
        # Cloudflare IP. Without this block `{client_ip}` resolves to that edge
        # IP for every request, which would:
        #   - collapse every visitor into ONE per-IP rate-limit bucket, so a
        #     single person tripping the login limit locks out all users
        #     (platform_shared.core.rate_limit keys off get_client_ip), and
        #   - write Cloudflare IPs into the auth-event audit log instead of
        #     real client IPs (platform_shared.services.auth_event_service).
        #
        # Declaring the Cloudflare ranges as trusted proxies makes Caddy skip
        # them when walking the forwarded chain. `client_ip_headers` then
        # prefers CF-Connecting-IP, which Cloudflare sets itself and strips
        # from inbound requests — so a client cannot forge it. X-Forwarded-For
        # stays as a fallback for any non-proxied path.
        #
        # Ranges from https://www.cloudflare.com/ips-v4 and /ips-v6, fetched
        # 2026-09-07. They change rarely. Deliberately kept on one line: a
        # Caddyfile parse error here takes ALL FIVE apps down at once, so this
        # trades diff readability for one less way to break the edge.
        trusted_proxies static 173.245.48.0/20 103.21.244.0/22 103.22.200.0/22 103.31.4.0/22 141.101.64.0/18 108.162.192.0/18 190.93.240.0/20 188.114.96.0/20 197.234.240.0/22 198.41.128.0/17 162.158.0.0/15 104.16.0.0/13 104.24.0.0/14 172.64.0.0/13 131.0.72.0/22 2400:cb00::/32 2606:4700::/32 2803:f800::/32 2405:b500::/32 2405:8100::/32 2a06:98c0::/29 2c0f:f248::/32
        client_ip_headers CF-Connecting-IP X-Forwarded-For
    }
}

# Shared TLS snippet — every proxied hostname uses the same Cloudflare Origin
# CA certificate. One cert covering myfreeapps.org + *.myfreeapps.org serves
# every app subdomain, so adding an app needs no certificate work.
#
# These files are installed on the VPS OUT OF BAND (see the PR's operational
# migration section) and are NOT in git — the .key is a private key. If they
# are missing, `caddy reload` fails and the previously-loaded config keeps
# serving, but a full restart would fail to come up. Install the cert BEFORE
# deploying this file.
(cloudflare_origin_tls) {
    tls /etc/caddy/cloudflare-origin.pem /etc/caddy/cloudflare-origin.key
}

# Shared edge security headers — network-level protections that belong at the
# TLS edge. Everything else lives in each app's docker Caddy.
(edge_headers) {
    header {
        Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains; preload"
        X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
        Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
        -Server
    }
}

# Shared reverse-proxy header handling. Overwrites the forwarded chain with
# Caddy's computed real client IP (derived from CF-Connecting-IP via the
# trusted-proxy config above). The docker Caddy trusts `private_ranges` and
# reads X-Forwarded-For, so this is the handoff point where the true client IP
# enters the app's view. Omit it and rate limiting silently keys on
# Cloudflare's edge IP instead of the visitor.
(proxy_client_ip) {
    header_up X-Forwarded-For {client_ip}
}

# MyBookkeeper — primary domain.
# The legacy 165-245-134-251.sslip.io address was dropped on 2026-09-07 with
# the Cloudflare cutover: that hostname literally encodes the droplet's IP, so
# keeping it published would let anyone bypass the proxy and reach the origin
# directly — defeating both the origin-hiding and the routing redundancy.
mybookkeeper.myfreeapps.org {
    import cloudflare_origin_tls
    import edge_headers

    reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8094 {
        import proxy_client_ip
    }
}

# Shared MinIO — public storage endpoint. The minio container binds to
# 127.0.0.1:9000 (see infra/docker-compose.yml). MinIO listens HTTP-only —
# host Caddy terminates TLS for storage.<domain> and proxies plaintext to
# MinIO. Backends sign presigned URLs against this hostname so the browser
# can fetch attachments directly without round-tripping through any API.
#
# Proxied through Cloudflare like everything else, so images and attachments
# keep loading for visitors on the affected routes. Traffic here is presigned
# GET only (platform_shared.core.storage uses presigned_get_object; there are
# no presigned PUTs), so Cloudflare's request-body limit does not apply —
# uploads go through /api/* on the app subdomains instead.
#
# REQUIRED companion config: a Cloudflare Cache Rule setting "Bypass cache"
# for hostname storage.myfreeapps.org. Presigned URLs carry their signature in
# the query string and expire (presigned_url_ttl_seconds, default 3600); a
# cached object could otherwise be replayed to a request whose signature has
# expired or differs. Verify that rule exists before trusting this block.
storage.myfreeapps.org {
    import cloudflare_origin_tls
    import edge_headers

    reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:9000 {
        import proxy_client_ip
    }
}

# MyJobHunter — separate subdomain.
myjobhunter.myfreeapps.org {
    import cloudflare_origin_tls
    import edge_headers

    reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8092 {
        import proxy_client_ip
    }
}

# MyGamingAssistant — separate subdomain.
# Public clip media is served separately by Cloudflare R2 at
# mga-clips.myfreeapps.org (a CNAME to R2, NOT proxied here) — see
# apps/mygamingassistant/backend/.env.docker.example MINIO_PUBLIC_BASE_URL.
# NOTE: that CNAME does not currently resolve in public DNS; confirm it during
# the Cloudflare zone import rather than assuming the scan carried it over.
mygamingassistant.myfreeapps.org {
    import cloudflare_origin_tls
    import edge_headers

    reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8096 {
        import proxy_client_ip
    }
}

# MyRecipes — separate subdomain.
myrecipes.myfreeapps.org {
    import cloudflare_origin_tls
    import edge_headers

    reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8100 {
        import proxy_client_ip
    }
}
